  abstract = {Electroglottography (EGG) is widely used to provide a direct and reliable physiological signal for pitch annotation. However, existing electroglottography-based GCI detection methods, including classical signal-processing baselines such aspeakdetandSIGMAand the CNN-basedgciclsbaseline, still face robustness and precision–recall tradeoff limitations under degraded electroglottographic recording conditions. This paper proposes EGGNet, a wide-context multi-branch convolutional model, which uses multi-scale convolutional modeling and a sequential refinement design for robust GCI detection. In addition, a cascaded supervision architecture is introduced as a training strategy to foster a collaborative mechanism between soft and hard labels, effectively balancing recall and localization accuracy. For evaluation, a tolerance-window criterion together with a greedy-based one-to-one matching strategy is adopted to mitigate temporal localization sparsity-induced distortions, eliminate the multi-to-one or one-to-multi evaluation bias common in traditional methods, and limit matching errors to$\pm 0.25$mssampling points amid tens of thousands of samples per second. Experiments trained on the PTDB-TUG dataset show that EGGNet outperforms the strongest external reference baseline, the CNN-basedgcicls, achieving F1 scores of 96.4%, 95.9%, and 80.4% on CSTR-FDA, KEELE, and MOCHA-TIMIT, respectively, corresponding to absolute F1 gains of 10.2, 1.3, and 1.7 percentage points overgcicls. Ablatio},
}

